{"input_text": "Question: Let  $X,Y$  be two independent random variables where  $X$  is standard normal distributed and  $Y$  is distributed as  $P(Y=1)=P(Y=-1)=\frac{1}{2}$   how can one calculate  $\mathbb{E}[e^{XY}]$ ? I don't see how to use the joinst density of  $X,Y$  to find the expectation (since one is continious and the other is discrete). I also don't see how I can factor the density since we dont have  $\mathbb{E}[f(X)g(Y)]=\mathbb{E}[f(X)]\mathbb{E}[g(Y)]$  but rather  $\mathbb{E}[h(X,Y)]$ . 

Answer: \begin{align}
\mathbb{E}\big[e^{XY}\big]&= \mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[e^{XY}\big|Y\right]\right]\\
&= \frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}\left[e^{XY}\big|Y=-1\right]+ \frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}\left[e^{XY}\big|Y=1\right]\\
&= \frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}\left[e^{-X}\right]+ \frac{1}{2}\mathbb{E}\left[e^{X}\right]\\
&=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^\infty\big(e^{-x}+e^x\big)e^{-\frac{x^2}{2}}dx\\
&= \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^\infty e^\frac{1}{2}\big(e^{-\frac{(x+1)^2}{2}}+e^{-\frac{(x-1)^2}{2}}\big)dx\\
&=e^\frac{1}{2}
\end{align} 
